    "threat": "Unencrypted message content in transit",
    "description": "RabbitMQ 3.13 supports AMQP 0-9-1 with TLS, but the connection uses Basic authentication without explicit TLS encryption indication beyond 'encrypted=true' — if TLS termination occurs at the broker but messages are not encrypted end-to-end, sensitive order and billing events could be exposed if any intermediate component (e.g., proxy, load balancer) strips TLS or misroutes traffic [ref:CWE-319]. The data classification (CONFIDENTIAL/RESTRICTED) requires encryption of data in transit across all hops, which is not guaranteed by the current configuration."
  },
  {
    "threat": "Default or weak access controls on queue resources",
    "description": "RabbitMQ 3.13 defaults to a 'guest' user with no password enforcement if not configured — and while the connection uses Basic auth, there is no documented access control policy for queues (e.g., vhost-level permissions, exchange binding restrictions). An attacker compromising the Order or Billing service could exploit missing fine-grained access controls to read or subscribe to queues containing billing events [ref:CWE-287]. Without explicit vhost isolation between order and billing queues, cross-service data leakage is possible [ref:CWE-284]."
  }
]

